                        <span class="section-eyebrow">Strategic Property Protection</span>
                        <h2 class="section-heading">Safeguarding Rental Investments for Overseas Property Owners</h2>
                        <p class="section-lead">
                            Managing valuable residential and commercial real estate in India while residing abroad presents significant legal and operational vulnerabilities.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Non-Resident Indians frequently confront uncooperative tenants who exploit geographic distance, time zone differences, and lack of direct physical oversight. Common tenant malpractices include <strong>chronic default on agreed monthly rents, unauthorized subletting to third parties, illegal structural modifications, and outright refusal to vacate</strong> upon lease expiration.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Historically, tenancy litigation in India was infamous for dragging through Civil Courts for decades under the archaic Tamil Nadu Buildings (Lease and Rent Control) Act of 1960. Recognizing these structural flaws, the Government of Tamil Nadu enacted the landmark <strong>Tamil Nadu Regulation of Rights and Responsibilities of Landlords and Tenants Act, 2017 (TNRRRLT Act 2017)</strong>, which came into full effect in February 2019.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Under this modern framework, dispute adjudication is transferred exclusively to designated <strong>Rent Courts (presided by Sub-Judges or District Munsifs) and Rent Tribunals (Principal District Judges)</strong>. The Act mandates summary trials, strictly curbs frivolous adjournments, establishes an expedited 90-day disposal timeline, and recognizes clear statutory eviction grounds — providing NRI landlords with an unprecedented legal advantage.
                        </p>
