        <section class="content-section" id="faq">
            <div class="container">
                <div class="text-center max-w-750 mx-auto mb-4">
                    <span class="section-eyebrow">Legal Insights</span>
                    <h2 class="section-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
                    <p class="section-lead">Clear, authoritative answers to essential questions regarding tenancy enforcement, eviction, and Rent Court practice in Tamil Nadu.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="accordion faq-accordion max-w-850 mx-auto" id="tenantFaqAccordion">

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="headingOne">
                            <button class="accordion-button" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseOne" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="collapseOne">
                                1. How long does it take to evict a defaulting tenant under the TNRRRLT Act 2017?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="collapseOne" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" aria-labelledby="headingOne" data-bs-parent="#tenantFaqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                Under the previous 1960 Rent Control Act, eviction suits routinely languished for years. In stark contrast, the <strong>TNRRRLT Act 2017</strong> mandates that Rent Courts follow summary trial procedures with a statutory target timeline of <strong>90 days from the date of service of summons</strong>. While contested matters involving appeals to the Rent Tribunal may require 6 to 9 months, summary eviction is drastically faster than ordinary civil suits.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="headingTwo">
                            <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseTwo" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapseTwo">
                                2. Is registration of tenancy agreements mandatory in Tamil Nadu, and what is the T-Number?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="collapseTwo" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="headingTwo" data-bs-parent="#tenantFaqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                Yes. Under Section 4 of the TNRRRLT Act 2017, all tenancy agreements (residential and commercial) must be reduced to writing and registered on the official Tamil Nadu Tenancy portal (<strong>tenancy.tn.gov.in</strong>). Once registered, the Rent Authority generates a unique <strong>Tenancy Registration Number (T-Number)</strong>. Having a T-Number provides prima facie statutory evidence of tenancy terms before the Rent Court.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="headingThree">
                            <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseThree" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapseThree">
                                3. What happens if an NRI landlord does not have a registered written rental agreement?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="collapseThree" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="headingThree" data-bs-parent="#tenantFaqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                Under Section 4(2) of the Act, if the landlord and tenant fail to execute a registered agreement within the statutory transitional window, either party may apply to the Rent Court. If the tenant refuses or fails to enter into a registered contract upon notice, the landlord is entitled to petition the Rent Court for summary repossession under <strong>Section 21(2)(a)</strong>. The absence of a registered lease works in favor of the landlord seeking repossession, rather than the tenant.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="headingFour">
                            <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseFour" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapseFour">
                                4. Can an NRI landlord evict a tenant without traveling to India?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="collapseFour" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="headingFour" data-bs-parent="#tenantFaqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                Yes, entirely. Non-Resident Indians (NRIs) and Overseas Citizens of India (OCIs) can manage the entire litigation remotely by executing a Special Power of Attorney (PoA) in favor of their advocate or trusted local representative. The PoA is attested at the nearest Indian Embassy or Consulate abroad, dispatched to India, and duly adjudicated before the jurisdictional District Registrar in Tamil Nadu. Our advocates represent you at every court hearing.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="headingFive">
                            <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseFive" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapseFive">
                                5. How much security deposit can a landlord legally collect in Tamil Nadu?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="collapseFive" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="headingFive" data-bs-parent="#tenantFaqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                Under Section 13(1) of the TNRRRLT Act 2017, the advance security deposit payable by a tenant is legally capped at a maximum of <strong>three months' agreed rent</strong> for residential premises, and up to six months' rent for commercial premises. The security deposit must be refunded to the tenant upon handing over vacant possession, subject to lawful deductions for rent arrears or documented physical damages.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="headingSix">
                            <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseSix" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapseSix">
                                6. Can a tenant claim adverse possession or ownership over rental property?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="collapseSix" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="headingSix" data-bs-parent="#tenantFaqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                No. Under Section 116 of the Indian Evidence Act, a tenant who enters property under a landlord is statutorily estopped from denying the landlord's title. Long permissive possession as a tenant, even across several decades or after lease expiry, can <strong>never mature into adverse possession</strong>. The Supreme Court of India has repeatedly held that a tenant's possession remains permissive until vacant possession is formally surrendered.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="headingSeven">
                            <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseSeven" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapseSeven">
                                7. What are the valid legal grounds for eviction under Section 21(2) of the Act?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="collapseSeven" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="headingSeven" data-bs-parent="#tenantFaqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                The primary grounds under Section 21(2) include: <strong>(a)</strong> default in payment of agreed rent, <strong>(b)</strong> refusal to vacate after expiry of the agreed period or termination notice, <strong>(c)</strong> unauthorized subletting of all or part of the premises, <strong>(d)</strong> substantial damage or unauthorized structural alteration, and <strong>(g)</strong> bona fide requirement of the premises for personal occupation by the landlord or their family members.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="headingEight">
                            <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseEight" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapseEight">
                                8. Can the landlord cut off electricity, water, or lock out a defaulting tenant?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="collapseEight" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="headingEight" data-bs-parent="#tenantFaqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                No, absolutely not. Under Section 20 of the TNRRRLT Act 2017, landlords are strictly prohibited from disconnecting or withholding essential services (such as water, electricity, or sanitation) or taking the law into their own hands through lockouts. Engaging in extrajudicial dispossession exposes the landlord to court injunctions and statutory fines. Eviction must always be conducted through the lawful mechanism of the Rent Court.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

